  "worked_solution": "Let's recall the functional representations of our three transformations:\n\n- A shift of $2$ units up can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,y+2).\n$$\n\n- A reflection in the $y$-axis can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(-x,y).\n$$\n\n- A shift of $1$ unit to the left can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x-1,y).\n$$\n\nLet's apply the given transformations to the point $Q$ using our functions:\n\nFirst, we apply the shift. The image of the point $(-3,1)$ under the shift is\n\n$$\n(-3,1)↦(-3,1+2)=(-3,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we apply the reflection. The image of the point $(-3,3)$ under the reflection is\n\n$$\n(-3,3)↦(3,3).\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the second shift by $1$ unit to the left. The image of the point $(3,3)$ under the shift is\n\n$$\n(3,3)↦(3-1,3)=(2,3).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(2,3).$",
